class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = dp[i-1] + 1;
            for (int j = i - 2; j <= i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n-1];
    }
}
```
In this solution, we maintain a `dp` array to store the length of the longest increasing subsequence within each window of elements. We initialize `dp[0] = 1` to indicate that the subsequence ending at index 0 has length 1 (it consists of only the element at index 0). We then iterate over the remaining elements in the array and calculate the length of the longest increasing subsequence that ends at each of these elements. If the current element is less than the next element in the window, we update `dp[i]` to be the maximum of `dp[i]` and the length of the longest increasing subsequence that ends at the current element plus 1.

This solution has a time complexity of O(n), which is much faster than the nested loops approach for large input sizes.

