```js
const div = document.querySelector('#div');
div.addEventListener('dragover', e => e.preventDefault());
div.addEventListener('dragenter', () => div.style.border = 'dashed 2px #ccc');
div.addEventListener('dragleave', () => div.style.border = ''); 
div.addEventListener('drop', e => {
  e.preventDefault();
  div.style.border = '';
  const rect = div.getBoundingClientRect();
  const x = e.clientX - rect.left;
  const y = e.clientY - rect.top;
  // x and y are the relative mouse coordinates
});
```

3. The drop event will fire when the element is dropped onto the div. In the handler, you can get the div's bounding client rect, and subtract it from the event's clientX and clientY to get the relative coordinates within the div.

4. Use the x and y coordinates to position the element as needed within the div.

