The time complexity of this function is O(n), which means that it runs in linear time with respect to the input integer. The space complexity is O(1), which means that it uses a constant amount of space to store the intermediate results.

Note: This code is just a simple example and may not work for large inputs. For more efficient factorial algorithms, you can use other methods such as the sieve of Eratosthenes, the GCD algorithm, or the Stirling's formula.

