You're right that not using a database can be a simpler and cost effective option for some use cases. To implement real-time location sharing with just WebSockets and Express, here's what you can do:

1. When a client connects to your WebSocket endpoint, have their socket join a "room" corresponding to their user ID or some other identifier. For example:

```js
socket.on('connect', () => {
  socket.join(socket.user_id);
});
```

2. When a location update is received from a client, NAME_1 it only to sockets in that client's room. For example: 

```js
socket.on('location_update', (location) => {
  io.in(socket.user_id).emit('location_update', location); 
});
```

3. Clients will not receive any historical updates. They will only get live updates from the time they connect.

4. You can have your server NAME_1 location updates to clients on a timer to simulate constant streaming of updates. For example:

```js
setInterval(() => {
  io.in(socket.user_id).emit('location_update', generateLocationUpdate());
}, 1000); 
```

5. When scaling to many clients, you'll want to implement a "fan-out" system where you have multiple WebSocket servers, a load balancer in front of them, and a message broker like Redis to help broadcast updates between servers.

Some pros of this approach are:

1. Simplicity - No database to manage 
2. Low cost - WebSockets and caching servers are very cost efficient

Some cons are:

1. No historical data - Newly connected clients won't get any location history 
2. Limited functionality - Can't run analytics, build historical APIs, etc without a database 
3. Scaling complexity - Requires more moving parts to scale to very high numbers of clients and servers

